<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Teke</h1><p class="st-meta">1321 CE – 1423 CE (approx.) · capital Antalya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish beylik on the Mediterranean coast around Antalya, a branch of the Hamidids that briefly lost its capital to the King of Cyprus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 14th century a branch of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hamidids">Hamidids</a> took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> and the surrounding coastland, known from its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> inhabitants as Teke. Around 1321 it became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya's</a> port, which traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Italian republics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in the 1330s and met its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khidr-bey-of-teke">Khidr Bey</a>. In 1361 Peter I of Cyprus captured Antalya, holding it until 1373, when the beylik regained it. The Teke rulers built mosques and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> in Antalya.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> annexed Teke around 1390, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> restored it in 1402. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-bey-of-teke">Osman Bey</a>, the last ruler, was killed besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> in 1423, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/teke.jpg" alt="Teke" width="320" height="162"><figcaption>Image: Lord Leatherface at Turkish Wikipedia — English translation by Morningstar1814 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.